---
/**
 * The homepage.
 *
 * SEO WAS CARRIED OVER VERBATIM from the live legacy homepage: title,
 * description, canonical and the Organization/WebSite JSON-LD graph. The design
 * changed; the metadata did not. Changing homepage metadata is a separate,
 * measured decision and must never ride along inside a redesign.
 *
 * THAT SEPARATE DECISION HAS NOW BEEN TAKEN TWICE, AND BOTH ARE RECORDED HERE.
 * The title and description below are still the ONLY two values that have moved
 * from the legacy baseline. The canonical, the JSON-LD graph and every other
 * page's metadata are untouched by either.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-03, OA-33: THE MARKET SCOPE CAME OUT ────────────────────────────
 *
 * "for UK suppliers" -> "for suppliers" was the whole change on the title, and
 * the description dropped one word. Both scoped the product to the UK, which
 * contradicts the market-neutral narrative the owner settled on 2026-08-02: UK
 * public sector is the PROOF POINT, not the identity, and the site has to cover
 * UK private, EU and global private tenders as well. OA-33 is about the six
 * headings and two metadata strings that scope the PRODUCT, not about the many
 * places UK correctly describes the SUBJECT — UK GDPR on /privacy, UK hosting
 * on /security, UK statute on /sources. The distinction OA-33 turns on is
 * whether UK describes the world or the product.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-05, A-112: THE SIDE SCOPE COMES OUT TOO ─────────────────────────
 *
 * Owner instruction, verbatim: "home page must be both as our bid suite is for
 * both supplier and buyer and also covering public and private RFP's as well,
 * must cover all". This DISCHARGES the caution at the top of this comment for
 * this one field pair: the change is now the owner's measured decision rather
 * than something riding along inside a redesign, so the caution no longer
 * stands against it and the header must not be left saying that it does.
 *
 * OA-33 took a MARKET scope out of the title and left a SIDE scope standing in
 * its place. "Bid and tender software for suppliers" described half the suite:
 * CrowMark for Buyers publishes requirements and locates the evidence for each
 * one across the responses received, and it never scores, because the
 * evaluation panel scores. The replacement wording is drawn from the "What is
 * CrowAgent?" answer in data/faq.ts rather than newly invented, so the homepage
 * and the /faq answer that feeds the FAQPage node say the same thing about the
 * same product instead of two things.
 *
 * THE TITLE IS 58 CHARACTERS, AND THE 60-CHARACTER CEILING IS THE REASON IT IS A
 * LIST. A-89 cut titles over 60 from 20 down to 4 and a fifth is not being added
 * here. The "CrowAgent | " prefix spends 12 of the 60, and naming four things
 * inside the remaining 48 is what the sentence forms cannot do. Measured, not
 * guessed:
 *
 *   58  CrowAgent | Bid software: supplier, buyer, public, private   <- chosen
 *   60  CrowAgent | Bids for suppliers and buyers, public or private
 *   61  CrowAgent | Bid AI for supplier and buyer, public and private
 *   64  CrowAgent | Bid software, supplier and buyer, public and private
 *
 * So a sentence CAN be got to 60, but only by giving up "software" and by
 * weakening "and" to "or", which reads as an either/or the owner's instruction
 * explicitly is not. The list keeps the search phrase and says "all four" rather
 * than "one of four", and it leaves two characters of headroom instead of none.
 * The description below carries the sentence the title cannot.
 *
 * OG AND TWITTER FOLLOW WITHOUT A PROP, WHICH IS WHY NONE IS PASSED BELOW.
 * components/seo/Seo.astro defaults ogTitle to title, ogDescription to
 * description, twitterTitle to ogTitle, twitterDescription to ogDescription and
 * the og/twitter image alt to description, so all six social fields move with
 * the two values below. Passing explicit ogTitle/ogDescription here would create
 * a second copy of the same sentence to keep in step, which is the exact defect
 * A-50 had to fix.
 *
 * TWO COPIES OF THE OLD DESCRIPTION SURVIVE OUTSIDE THIS FILE AND A-112 DOES
 * NOT TOUCH THEM: data/site.ts organizationSchema.description, which ships in
 * the @graph on every route, and data/footer.ts brand.tagline, which renders in
 * the footer on every route. A-50 aligned both to this file's wording on
 * 2026-08-04 precisely so the visible copy and the machine-readable graph could
 * not disagree, and that alignment is broken from the moment the sentence below
 * changed. Both files were outside the scope this change was made under, so the
 * divergence is handed back written down rather than edited quietly.
 *
 * check-seo-parity.js compares this against the legacy baseline and will report
 * BOTH `title` and `description` as changed on `/`. That is the gate doing its
 * job: it fails only on a field that has been LOST and reports a changed value
 * for a human to read. Recording the change here with its reason is the answer;
 * weakening the gate is not.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import HeroStack from '../components/sections/HeroStack.astro';
import ProofGate from '../components/sections/ProofGate.astro';
/* Imported in the order they render. See the section map below: MarketShape is
   section 3 from 2026-08-05 and ProductScreens is section 4. */
import MarketShape from '../components/sections/MarketShape.astro';
import ProductScreens from '../components/sections/ProductScreens.astro';
import HowItRuns from '../components/sections/HowItRuns.astro';
import BothSides from '../components/sections/BothSides.astro';
import ReasoningTrace from '../components/sections/ReasoningTrace.astro';
import Integrations from '../components/sections/Integrations.astro';
import FinalCta from '../components/sections/FinalCta.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

/* 58 characters. See A-112 in the header for why this is a list and not a
   sentence, and for the 60-character ceiling A-89 set. */
const title = 'CrowAgent | Bid software: supplier, buyer, public, private';
const description =
  'CrowAgent serves both sides of a procurement, public sector and private. Suppliers answer tenders, RFPs, PQQs and SQs from bids already written. Buyers publish requirements and find the evidence.';

/** The WebSite node the legacy page emits alongside Organization. */
const webSite = {
  '@type': 'WebSite',
  '@id': `${SITE.origin}/#website`,
  url: `${SITE.origin}/`,
  name: SITE.name,
  publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.origin}/#organization` },
};
---

{/*
  ── THE NINE SECTIONS, IN THE OWNER'S ORDER ─────────────────────────────────

  ORDER SET 2026-08-04; SECTION 3 MOVED 2026-08-05 UNDER A-104 ITEM 1.

  Written down here because the order IS the argument and the next person to
  open this file should be able to see the whole of it without opening nine
  components. Owner instruction, in the owner's own numbering, as it now stands:

    1  Hero, UNCHANGED           HeroStack        as it shipped, not one word
    2  The two cards             ProofGate        NEW, from the concept's hero
    3  Statutory figures         MarketShape      MOVED UP 2026-08-05, A-104 (1)
    4  The product               ProductScreens   NEW, from the concept
    5  How it runs               HowItRuns        NEW, from the concept
    6  Both sides of the table   BothSides        live, plus a standfirst
    7  Run the real engine       ReasoningTrace   live, mixed with the concept's
                                                  "core principle"
    8  Plugged in, read only     Integrations     live, untouched
    9  Ready when you are        FinalCta         live, untouched

  ── WHY STATUTORY FIGURES MOVED, 2026-08-05, A-104 ITEM 1 ───────────────────

  Owner instruction, verbatim: "STATUTORY FIGURES MOVES ABOVE THE PRODUCT,
  expands into the page, and loses most of its animation - this breaks the
  floor/base surface alternation and it must be re-derived." Only the move is
  recorded here; the expansion and the animation are the section's own business
  and are handled in MarketShape.astro, and the alternation is re-derived in the
  block below this one because the alternation is the PAGE's business and never
  a component's.

  THE SHAPE OF IT, RESTATED FOR THE NEW ORDER AND NOT LEFT DESCRIBING THE OLD.
  The hero says what the product does. Section 2 says where it is proved and
  what it refuses. Section 3 now gives the four published figures the reader is
  already held to, BEFORE any screen is shown, so the duties are the frame the
  product is then read inside rather than a footnote arriving after it. Section
  4 shows the product against that frame. Section 5 says how it runs. Sections 6
  and 7 are the two arguments that need a drawing. Section 8 states the limits
  of what we read, and section 9 asks.

  ── ONE LIVE SECTION IS NOT IN THAT LIST, AND IT IS GONE FROM THIS PAGE ─────

  Lifecycle, the four-stage ring headed "Delivery is the first stage of the next
  bid." The owner's order has no slot for it and HowItRuns is what stands where
  it stood: the same claim, five stages instead of four, with the refusal named
  as one of them. Keeping both would put two "here is the whole engine" diagrams
  three sections apart.

  IT COSTS NOTHING IN CONTENT PARITY, WHICH WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The
  five legacy headings that section answered for are all already recorded as
  deliberate losses in scripts/check-content-parity.js, and their reasons have
  been rewritten to point at this section instead of at the ring. The component
  file is left on disk rather than deleted: nothing else imports it, and a
  drawing that took a Figma frame to specify should not be thrown away in the
  same pass that stops using it.

  ── THE GROUND LADDER, RE-DERIVED 2026-08-05 · A-104 ITEM 1 AND A-118 ───────

  These are the same job seen from two sides. A-104 item 1 says moving section 3
  "breaks the floor/base surface alternation and it must be re-derived"; A-118
  says the containers "alternate between panelled and bare with no codified
  rule" and asks for one pattern defined once and applied. ONE RULE ANSWERS
  BOTH, and it is stated here in words before it is written as CSS below:

    THE GROUND OF A SECTION IS A FUNCTION OF ITS POSITION AND OF NOTHING ELSE.
    Odd-numbered sections are FLOOR and paint nothing, so the --c-floor declared
    on <html> shows through. Even-numbered sections are BASE and paint --c-bg,
    one step lighter. No section is exempt and no section states its own ground.

  So the page now runs, top to bottom:

    1  HeroStack       odd    FLOOR   --c-floor via <html>, unpainted
    2  ProofGate       even   BASE    --c-bg
    3  MarketShape     odd    FLOOR   --c-floor via <html>, unpainted
    4  ProductScreens  even   BASE    --c-bg
    5  HowItRuns       odd    FLOOR   --c-floor via <html>, unpainted
    6  BothSides       even   BASE    --c-bg
    7  ReasoningTrace  odd    FLOOR   --c-floor via <html>, unpainted
    8  Integrations    even   BASE    --c-bg
    9  FinalCta        OUTSIDE THE LADDER. It is a <div class="fc surface">, not
                       a <section>, so the selector below cannot reach it and it
                       carries its own card ground, as it always has.

  WHY POSITION AND NOT A CLASS PER COMPONENT, WHICH IS WHAT WAS THERE BEFORE.
  Six components each hard-coded `class="sv-floor"` or the absence of it, so the
  alternation existed only as a coincidence between six files and this one, and
  moving a single section silently broke it in two places at once — that is
  precisely the defect A-104 item 1 reports. Position is the one fact only this
  file knows, so this file is the only place the rule can live. Reorder the
  sections below and the ladder follows them; nothing else has to be edited, and
  nothing can drift out of step with it.

  AND THE OLD MECHANISM WAS ALREADY DEAD, MEASURED ON THE BUILT PAGE AT 1440
  BEFORE ANY OF THIS CHANGED. Every one of the eight sections reported
  rgb(3, 4, 10). `.sv-floor` paints --c-floor, and A-103 moved --c-floor onto
  <html> earlier the same day, so the class became a no-op painting the ground
  it was meant to step off: four sections declaring a floor, four declaring
  nothing, and one flat colour down the whole page. There was no alternation
  left to preserve, which is why this is a re-derivation rather than a reshuffle.

  THE TWO VALUES ARE THE CONCEPT'S, IN THE CONCEPT'S PHASE. concepts/homepage-v2
  .html puts --c-floor on <html>, leaves `.band` unpainted and paints --c-bg on
  `.band-inset`; measured on it, `.band` reports rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) over an <html>
  of rgb(3, 4, 10) and `.band-inset` reports rgb(5, 7, 14). That is exactly the
  pair below. Base.astro's note that "the ground is --c-bg on html and body" is
  the pre-A-103 arrangement and no longer describes this page.

  UNPAINTED RATHER THAN --c-floor ON THE ODD RUNGS, AND IT IS NOT A SHORTCUT. It
  is what the concept does, and it is what keeps the ambient rigs working: <body>
  is transparent by design so a ramp can paint BETWEEN it and the root, and an
  opaque section ground would cover that ramp. specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 asks
  that a section be floor or base and never ambiguous; a section the rule below
  assigns to floor is not ambiguous, it is assigned.

  WHAT THIS MAKES REDUNDANT, AND WHAT MUST NOT BE ASSUMED FROM THAT. The
  `class="sv-floor"` still declared inside ProofGate, HowItRuns, BothSides and
  Integrations no longer decides anything on THIS route: the rule below is
  unlayered and beats `.sv-floor`, which sits in @layer components. Those four
  components were locked to other agents while this was written, so the dead
  declarations are handed back written down rather than edited quietly. They
  cannot be deleted blind either — a component used on another route may still
  be relying on its own ground there, and that has to be checked per component
  rather than assumed.
*/}
<style is:global>
  /*
   * THE HOMEPAGE GROUND LADDER. The rule is argued in full in the comment
   * directly above this element; what follows is only its two lines.
   *
   * `#main-content > section` is the eight ladder sections and nothing else:
   * the direct-child combinator keeps every nested <section> out, and FinalCta
   * is a <div> so it is out by construction rather than by exclusion.
   *
   * UNLAYERED ON PURPOSE. styles/surfaces.css declares `.sv-floor` inside
   * @layer components, and an unlayered rule beats any layered one whatever its
   * specificity, so this wins over a component's own ground without a fight and
   * without an !important. Verified against the built page rather than reasoned
   * about: with these two lines injected, section 5 (which still carries
   * `sv-floor`) resolved to transparent and sections 2, 4, 6 and 8 resolved to
   * rgb(5, 7, 14).
   *
   * `is:global` because the elements it addresses are rendered by nine other
   * components, none of which Astro's scoping would let this file reach. It is
   * still only this page's stylesheet: Astro bundles a page's styles per route,
   * so nothing here ships to the other 42.
   */
  #main-content > section:nth-of-type(odd) {
    background-color: transparent;
  }

  #main-content > section:nth-of-type(even) {
    background-color: var(--c-bg);
  }
</style>
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/" schema={[webSite]}>
  <HeroStack />
  <ProofGate />
  {/* Section 3 from 2026-08-05, A-104 item 1. It was section 5. The ground
      ladder above is derived from this order, so moving anything here moves the
      surfaces with it and neither has to be edited twice. */}
  <MarketShape />
  <ProductScreens />
  <HowItRuns />
  <BothSides />
  <ReasoningTrace />
  {/*
    INTEGRATIONS SITS HERE, WHICH IS WHERE THE LIVE SITE PUTS IT: after the
    reasoning trace and before the closing call to action (index.html at the
    repo root, section 6, between the engine showcase and the CTA banner). The
    order is also the argument, though not in the way this comment first said.
    It described the section spending the orchid refusal signal on a struck
    return arrow. That was true of IN-A; the owner chose IN-C, a chip grid,
    which has no arrow to strike.

    The ordering still earns its place, and for a better reason. The reasoning
    trace has just shown the engine refusing a figure it cannot trace. This
    section then names, per connector, the exact scope we hold: Sites.Read.All,
    Files.Read.All, sign-in only. A reader who has watched the engine refuse
    something reads a published scope as a limit we accept rather than a feature
    we advertise. Moving it above the trace would make the same list read as
    capability.
  */}
  <Integrations />
  {/* Every homepage section is now ported. Sections 3 and 5 were the last two,
      both gated on OA-01: an unsupported uniqueness claim, and 40/30/20%
      weightings presented as a published breakdown with no authority named.
      Both were fixed at source before porting, so neither claim moved onto the
      new homepage. */}
  {/*
    THE COPY MOVED FROM THE COMPONENT TO THE CALL SITE, 2026-08-03, AND NOT ONE
    WORD CHANGED. FinalCta is now the site's one CTA card and serves every route,
    so homepage copy hard-coded inside it would have been homepage copy on 24
    pages. The two trust signals its header comment protects — "we will run it
    with you" and "Access is limited and by request" — are both still here.
  */}
  {/*
    ── THE TWO FREE LINKS, DOWN TO ONE, 2026-08-04 ───────────────────────────

    They were `/tools/ppn-002-calculator/` and its `/methodology/` sub-page. The
    owner has had the PPN 002 calculator removed outright — "not giving any
    value" — and both routes are off disk, so left alone this card would have
    shipped two 404s from the homepage.

    ONE LINK, NOT TWO, AND THE SECOND IS DROPPED RATHER THAN AIMED SOMEWHERE.
    The replacement free tool is the Tender Compliance Matrix, and it is not the
    calculator under a new name: the calculator did arithmetic on a weighting,
    the matrix reads tender wording and returns the requirements that wording
    states. "Score a bid, free" is not what it does, so the label is rewritten
    rather than repointed.

    "How scoring works" led to a methodology page explaining where the
    calculator's proxy figures came from. The matrix has no such page and does
    not need one: it publishes its four rules in full, on its own page, for the
    same reason — a tool that reports what a document says has to be checkable.
    /glossary/ppn-002 still carries the 10% weighting fact, but a glossary entry
    is not a methodology, and pointing a closing call to action at one to keep a
    pair would be aiming a link somewhere unrelated. One honest link beats two.
  */}
  <FinalCta
    eyebrow="Ready when you are"
    title="See it on your own tender."
    standfirst="Bring a live notice and we will run it with you. Access is limited and by request."
    id="final-cta"
    links={[{ label: 'Read a tender, free', href: '/tools/tender-compliance-matrix/' }]}
  >
    {/*
      ── ONE PRIMARY, AND "BOOK A DEMO" IS NOT COMING BACK, A-117, 2026-08-05 ──

      This card used to render TWO buttons side by side: "Request access" to
      /contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form and a secondary "Book a demo"
      to /contact. Two labels, one destination. The second one asked the reader
      to choose between two names for the same page, and a choice with no
      consequence is a cost with no benefit at the exact point the page is
      asking for something.

      "Request access" is the label that survives, sitewide, because it is the
      one that is TRUE here: access is gated by request, and the standfirst
      directly above says so in as many words. The removed button said "Book a
      demo" and went to the contact form, not to the Calendly booking that the
      same label opens on /crowmark, /sectors and /tools, so it promised a
      booking and delivered a form. Two wrong things, not one.

      THE FREE LINK BESIDE IT IS NOT A SECOND CTA AND STAYS. "Read a tender,
      free" points at /tools/tender-compliance-matrix/, which needs no account
      and is a genuinely different offer rather than a second name for this one.
      Dropping a duplicate is not the same as dropping the alternative.

      THE HERO'S OWN PAIR IS UNTOUCHED, and not only because A-117 scoped it
      out. Its secondary is "Learn more" to /crowmark, a different destination
      answering a different question, so it is not the defect this item is
      about. Do not "finish the job" there.
    */}
    <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
  </FinalCta>
</Base>

{/*
  THE ONLY SCRIPT ON THIS PAGE, AND IT CANNOT BREAK IT.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every section above is complete, readable and at full contrast before this
  loads. All this module does is set data-lit="on" on five elements as they
  come into view, which lets CSS run a light across drawings that are already
  finished. If it 404s, throws, or is blocked, nothing on the homepage is
  missing: the reader gets exactly the rest frame each section's Figma board
  specifies. That inversion is the whole design, and it is the correction to
  sv-reveal, which started content at opacity 0 and left 4 of 9 sections on
  this page permanently invisible when its observer did not fire.

  It is booted here, once, rather than from a layout, because it is the
  homepage's motion and no other route carries a data-lit element. Astro
  bundles this into a single hashed module and defers it.

  THE HERO IS NOT IN IT. The hero's key light loops, is pure CSS inside a
  clipped 2px rail, and is above the fold on every viewport, so asking an
  observer whether it is visible is a question with a known answer and a
  failure mode.
*/}
<script>
  import { initMotion } from '../scripts/motion';

  initMotion();
</script>
